<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anxi Protectorate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">640–790</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Protectorate General to Pacify the West</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anxi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, or Protectorate General to Pacify the West, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>'s military government for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and the lands farther west. Founded in 640 at Jiaohe after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, it moved to Kucha in 648 and oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-garrisons-of-anxi">Four Garrisons of Anxi</a>. Weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> and Tibetan attacks, it was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> around 790.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anxi-protectorate.jpg" alt="Anxi Protectorate" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lệ Xuân · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_China_under_Emperor_Gaozong.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 19 September 640 the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-junji">Hou Junji</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> and set up Xi Prefecture in its place, and on 11 October it became the seat of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, with Qiao Shiwang as the first protector general. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a>, known to the Chinese as Yanqi, was conquered by 644, and in 648 the Tang took Yanqi and Qiuci (Kucha). The Four Garrisons of Kucha, Khotan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and Karashahr were set up between 648 and 658. In 658, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-dingfang">Su Dingfang</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, the office became a Grand Protectorate governing former Western Turkic lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>'s main rival was the Tibetan Empire. In 670 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> routed a Tang army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river">Battle of Dafei River</a> and captured Qiuci, and the seat moved for a time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. Tang troops withdrew from the Four Garrisons in 686 to save money, and the Tibetans took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> in 687, until in 692 Wang Xiaojie retook them and re-established the protectorate at Qiuci, where it remained to its end. In 717 the Tang beat an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a> by Turgesh, Arab and Tibetan forces.</p><p>In 751 the Tang suffered a major defeat against Abbasid and Karluk forces at the Battle of Talas, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>, which began in 755, is now understood as the main cause of the Tang retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The dynasty withdrew 200,000 soldiers to defend the capital, and in 764 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> and cut Anxi off. Guo Xin held on, and in 780 he was officially made protector general after sending secret messages to Emperor Dezong. In 789 the monk Wukong still found Chinese commanders in the garrisons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tang-era Chinese coins went on being copied and minted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and the Chu valley after the Chinese left. The Turkic name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Tamghaj, has been explained by the Tang's military dominance, and the Kara-Khanid khans called themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tabgach</a>. Among the Buddhist Uyghurs of the Kingdom of Qocho, Tang names appear on more than 50 temples, and when the Uyghurs developed the Old Uyghur alphabet they deliberately wrote it vertically, like Chinese. Muslim writers long afterwards counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, and sometimes Transoxania, as part of China.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Protectorate General to Pacify the West” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tang conquered Kucha in 648 and made it the seat of the <b>Anxi Protectorate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kucha">Kingdom of Kucha</a></li></ul></section>
